<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coricancha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qurikancha, Temple of the Sun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Coricancha was the most important temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, dedicated chiefly to the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>. Its walls of finely fitted stone were covered with sheets of gold, which the Spaniards stripped in 1533. The Dominican convent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> was built on its foundations, and parts of the Inca walls survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coricancha.jpg" alt="Coricancha" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coricancha,_Cusco,_Per%C3%BA,_2015-07-31,_DD_68.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Coricancha, from the Cusco Quechua Quri Kancha, meaning golden temple, was originally named Intikancha or Intiwasi and was dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> sun god. It stood in Cusco, then the capital of the Inca Empire, at the confluence of two rivers, one of them the Huatanay. According to Inca myth, Manco Cápac decided to build it there, as the foundation of Cusco and of the empire.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> built it of ashlar masonry, using cuboid stones cut by hand and shaped to a similar size, and rejected any stone with a slight imperfection or break. This labour was costly, and the technique came to signify the Inca's imperial power to mobilise labour. Early Spanish accounts say the stones fitted so well that no joining or cement could be seen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The high priest lived in the temple and offered the ordinary sacrifices with other priests. Pachakuti Inca Yupanqui rebuilt the House of the Sun, adding oracles, buildings and plates of fine gold, and supplied gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> vessels for the Mama-cunas, cloistered women who mostly wove, dyed wool and made chicha. The walls were once covered in sheets of gold, and the adjacent courtyard held golden statues.</p><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti-raymi">Inti Raymi</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuraka">curacas</a> went from the Haucaypata, where they greeted the rising June solstice sun, to the inner court. The Sapa Inca sat with his ancestors' mummies in the sun room, and used a concave mirror to focus the sun's rays and light the fire for burnt sacrifices of llamas. Children were sacrificed in certain circumstances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>When the Spanish demanded a ransom in gold for the life of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> in 1533, most of the gold was collected from the Coricancha. Most of the temple was destroyed after the war with the Spanish conquistadors, and settlers took it apart to build churches and houses. Its stonework became the foundation of the Church and Convent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, and construction took most of a century. Major earthquakes have damaged the church, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> walls still stand. Four rooms of the ancient temple with sloping walls remain in the convent, and an underground archaeological museum nearby holds mummies, textiles and sacred idols from the site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Coricancha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion centred on the sun god Inti, honoured at the <b>Coricancha</b> in Cusco, alongside the creator Viracocha, local sacred places called huacas, and the mummies of past rulers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cusco valley held 328 huacas, which were arranged along 41 or 42 radial pathways called ceques, all running out from the <b>Coricancha</b>, and the ritual duties for each shrine were recorded on quipus.</blockquote><a href="/religion/inca-religion">Inca religion · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…served the Sun, the Moon, the Thunder and the creator god Viracocha in Cusco, above all at the <b>Coricancha</b>, the gold-clad temple of the Sun, and at the state temples the Incas built in conquered provinces, while every people kept its own priests for its local shrines (wak’a),…</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spaniards later rebuilt its palaces and temples, including the <b>Coricancha</b>, on Inca foundations.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-15">15 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
